Explanation: The previous top‑scoring policies all used a small positive weight on cart position (≈ 0.5‑0.6), which amplified the natural drift of the cart away from the center, causing premature failures despite good angle control. By flipping this weight to a negative value we introduce a restoring force proportional to the cart’s displacement: when the cart moves right (positive x) the policy now applies a leftward push, and vice‑versa, directly counteracting the position drift. The strong positive pole‑angle weight (≈ 5.9) is retained because, combined with the existing negative cart‑velocity weight (‑0.3) and modest angular‑velocity gain (1.5), it still yields sufficient corrective torque to keep the pole within the ±0.2 rad health bound. Keeping the bias at zero avoids systematic offsets. This sign configuration should reduce cart‑position violations while preserving the good angle‑stabilization observed in the highest‑scoring examples, potentially extending episode length toward the 1000‑step optimum.
